.TH "SHELL\-LIKE ARITHMETIC EXPANSION" "3" "" "pstring 1.5" "pstring manual"
.SH NAME
Shell\-like arithmetic expansion \- pstreval parses and evaluates a single arithmetic expression using 64\-bit signed integer arithmetic throughout (matching bash's own $((...)), which has no floating point).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fB#include <eval.h>\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBtypedef int pstreval_get_fn(long long *out, const pstring_t *name, void *user);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBtypedef int pstreval_set_fn(const pstring_t *name, long long value, void *user);\fR
.fi
.PP
.nf
\fBint pstreval(
    long long *out,
    const pstring_t *expr,
    pstreval_get_fn *get,
    pstreval_set_fn *set,
    void *user
);\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBpstreval\fR parses and evaluates a single arithmetic expression using
64\-bit signed integer arithmetic throughout (matching \fBbash\fR's own
\fB$((...))\fR, which has no floating point). Supported grammar, from
lowest to highest precedence:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB,\fR (comma, evaluates both sides, yields the right)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB= += \-= *= /= %= &= ^= |= <<= >>=\fR (assignment, right\-associative)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB?:\fR (ternary, right\-associative)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB||\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB&&\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB|\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB^\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB&\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB== !=\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB< <= > >=\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB<< >>\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB+ \-\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB* / %\fR
.IP \(bu 2
unary \fB+ \- ! ~\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB( expr )\fR
.IP \(bu 2
integer literals: decimal, \fB0x\fR/\fB0X\fR hex, \fB0\fR + octal digits
.IP \(bu 2
identifiers, resolved via the \fBresolve\fR/\fBassign\fR callbacks
.PP
Division and modulo by zero are reported as \fBPSTRING_ERANGE\fR rather
than invoking undefined behaviour.
.SH REFERENCE
.SS long
.PP
Called for every bare identifier the expression references. Return
\fBPSTRING_OK\fR and write the variable's current value to \fB*out\fR (\fB0\fR if
unset is a reasonable default, matching shell semantics), or return an
error to abort evaluation. May be \fBNULL\fR, in which case every
identifier evaluates to \fB0\fR.
.SS const
.PP
Called when the expression assigns to an identifier (\fBx = 1\fR, \fBx += 2\fR,
a leading/trailing \fB++\fR/\fB\-\-\fR, ...). May be \fBNULL\fR, in which case
assignment expressions fail with \fBPSTRING_EINVAL\fR.
.SS pstreval
.PP
Evaluates \fBexpr\fR and writes the result to \fB*out\fR. \fBget\fR/\fBset\fR resolve
and store variable references; either may be \fBNULL\fR per their
documentation above. \fBuser\fR is passed through to both unchanged.
.PP
no \fBset\fR callback), ERANGE (division or modulo by zero), plus
whatever \fBget\fR/\fBset\fR themselves return.
.PP
